course: everyday
lesson: 468
title: Nice alone, mean in a group
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: On the walk home Chloe's friend Bella is lovely: shares her sweets, asks about Chloe's nan. At school, in front of the popular girls, the same Bella says, «Ew, why are you following me?» «Bestie, you look sooo good today,» she tells Chloe one morning — then to the others, «Lol did you see her shoes, they're giving charity shop.» Chloe's real friend Aisha hears it and tells her. Chloe feels like she has two friends with the same face. A friend who is kind only in private, and mean when there's an audience, is called a frenemy — half friend, half enemy.
explain: Kieran and Sam are mates on the street. At school, when the big lads are around, Kieran calls Sam «Sam-wich» and everyone laughs. «It's just banter, innit,» he says afterwards — banter means jokey teasing. On the walk home Sam finally says, «It's not banter if only one of us is laughing.» Kieran goes quiet, and mostly stops. Another week Kieran pretends not to know Sam when the cool kids appear, and Sam does exactly the same back next time, out of revenge. Neither feels good. They sit on the wall and admit it's daft.
explain: Mia's friend Harper is sweet at sleepovers, then posts Mia's sleepover photo in the cool kids' group chat with crying-laughing emojis. Mia asks her to take it down. «Chill, it's a joke,» says Harper — and deletes it only after Mia's mum talks to Harper's mum. Lily's friend Grace is nice one-to-one, but when Maddie's around Grace whispers and they both giggle and look at Lily. Lily asks Maddie straight: «Were you laughing at me?» Maddie says sorry — Grace had been saying mean stuff — and stops joining in.
explain: In Dundee, Lily's cousin says her friend Kirsty is «pure sound» on her own but «a right nippy sweetie» in a group. In Scots «pure sound» means really nice and a «nippy sweetie» is someone sharp-tongued. «Aye, ye just stick wi' her when she's on her own, ken?» — ken means «you know». Jake is friendly to Connor at football but trips him in the corridor in front of his older mates; the coach tells Jake to be «the same lad on and off the pitch». Connor's «best mate» Ryan is only his mate at home; at school Ryan's gang calls him «Con-fused». Connor's mum says, «A friend who's embarrassed to be seen with you isn't the friend you need, love.» Maddie notices her friend Ellie ignores her whenever the netball team's around; she tells Ellie, and Ellie, who hadn't noticed, says sorry and starts saying hi even with the team. And Lily's mum admits there's a woman at work who's sweet in the kitchen and moans about her in meetings: «Grown-ups have frenemies too, pet.»
fact: A frenemy is someone who acts like a friend in some places and unkind in others.
fact: Behaviour that changes depending on who is watching is a warning sign in a friendship.
fact: Compliments to your face and mean comments behind your back mean the friendship isn't real.
fact: Teasing is only banter if everyone, including the person teased, is laughing.
fact: Saying calmly and privately «I don't like it when you do that in front of them» gives a real friend the chance to stop.
fact: Paying a frenemy back with the same unkindness usually makes two frenemies and two unhappy people.
fact: Sharing someone's private photo to laugh at them is not a joke; ask for it to be removed and tell a grown-up.
fact: Asking a friend directly whether they were laughing at you can bring out the truth, because sometimes a friend is being used against you.
fact: Sometimes people don't notice how they change in a group, and telling them can fix it.
fact: A coach or teacher can help when someone is kind in one place and unkind in another.
fact: You deserve friends who are proud to be seen with you; clubs and other groups can help you find them.
fact: Grown-ups have frenemies too; spotting one is a skill for life.
fact: «Banter» is British for jokey teasing; in Scotland «pure sound» means really nice, a «nippy sweetie» is a sharp-tongued person, and «ken» means «you know».
exercise: What is a frenemy? | Someone who is a friend in some places and unkind in others | Someone who used to be your enemy but is now your friend for good | An enemy who never speaks to you | A friend from another school
exercise: How was Bella different on the walk home and at school? | Kind alone, mean in front of the popular girls | Mean on the walk home, but kind to her at school | Kind in both places | Mean in both places
exercise: Bella says «you look sooo good» then laughs at Chloe's shoes. What does that show? | The friendship isn't real | Bella is just being honest | Chloe's shoes really are bad | Bella was talking about someone else
exercise: How did Chloe find out what Bella said about her shoes? | Her real friend Aisha told her | Bella told her herself, laughing | She read it all in Bella's secret diary | A teacher announced it to the class
exercise: When is teasing really banter? | When everyone, including the teased person, is laughing | When the teaser says «it's banter» and doesn't mean anything by it | When it happens at school | When the big lads are watching
exercise: What did Sam say to Kieran that made him mostly stop? | «It's not banter if only one of us is laughing» | «I'll get the big lads on you if you say it once more» | «I'm telling everyone your nickname» | «You're not my mate any more»
exercise: Sam pretended not to know Kieran out of revenge. How did it end? | Both felt bad and admitted it was daft | Sam felt great and won | Kieran became Sam's best friend at once | The big lads made Sam king
exercise: Harper posted Mia's sleepover photo to laugh at her. What should Mia do? | Ask her to take it down and tell a grown-up | Post a worse photo of Harper in the same group chat | Leave the group chat forever | Say nothing and hope it goes away
exercise: How did Lily find out the truth about Grace and Maddie? | She asked Maddie straight | She spied on them at break | She read all of their messages | Grace confessed it all to her
exercise: What did Maddie do after Lily asked her? | Said sorry and stopped joining in | Laughed even harder | Joined Grace's side | Told Lily to stop being sensitive
exercise: «She's pure sound on her own but a right nippy sweetie in a group.» What is she like in a group? | Sharp-tongued | Very sweet | Very quiet and shy | Very cold
exercise: What does «ken» mean at the end of a Scottish sentence? | You know | Goodbye | Kenneth | Maybe not
exercise: Jake trips Connor at school but is friendly at football. What did the coach say? | Be the same lad on and off the pitch | Trip him at football too, so it's fair | Football and school don't ever mix | Stop playing with Connor
exercise: What did Connor's mum say about Ryan? | A friend embarrassed by you isn't the friend you need | Ryan will come round in the end if you just wait patiently | Make Ryan jealous | Tell Ryan's mum straight away
exercise: Ellie ignores Maddie in front of the netball team. How was it fixed? | Maddie told Ellie, and Ellie said sorry | Maddie quit netball and joined another team | Maddie ignored the whole team back | Ellie's mum made them hug and make up
exercise: Do grown-ups have frenemies? | Yes, frenemies happen at every age | No, only children do, grown-ups grow out of it | Only teachers have them | Only at weekends
test: Kieran after calling Sam «Sam-wich» in front of everyone: «chill, it's bants». Is it? | No, not if only one of them is laughing | Yes, if he says it's banter | Yes, Sam-wich is a funny name | No, banter only happens online
test: «Harper said it's just a joke so Mia's being dramatic». What's true? | Posting a private photo to laugh at someone isn't a joke | Harper's right, it's harmless if she says it's only a joke | Mia should laugh along | Photos can't hurt anyone
test: Sam thinks: «Kieran ignored me, so I'll ignore him in front of my mates, that'll teach him». What would most likely happen? | Two people feel bad and nothing is fixed | Kieran learns and says sorry at once | Sam becomes popular | The cool kids give Sam a medal
test: Lily sees Grace and Maddie giggling and looking at her. What's the best first step? | Ask Maddie directly, in private | Start giggling about them back, louder | Shout at both of them | Leave the school
test: «bella's lush on the walk home so she's my bestie, the school stuff doesn't count». Is that right? | No, a real friend is kind everywhere | Yes, only the walks home really count | Yes, school doesn't matter | No, walks home don't count
test: In a cartoon, the mean popular girl turns kind forever after one hug. What's more realistic? | Talking it through, or spending time with kinder friends | One hug fixes everything | Frenemies never change at all | A magic spell is needed
test: Connor: «Ryan's my best mate, he just can't be seen with me at school». What would his mum say? | You deserve a friend who's proud to be seen with you | That's normal for best mates, everyone does it at school | Pay Ryan to be nice at school | Just hide at school too
test: Your mate is friendly at home but «doesn't know you» when the cool kids walk past. What could you say? | «I don't like it when you do that in front of them» | «You're dead to me forever, so don't ever talk to me» | Nothing ever, just put up with it | «I'll do it to you double»
test: Lily's cousin says «stick wi' her when she's on her own, ken». What's the advice? | Enjoy her alone but don't trust the group version | Join her group and be mean too, so she's nicer to you | Never see her again | Tell her mum about her
test: «only kids have frenemies, grown-ups are past all that». What did Lily's mum say? | Grown-ups have frenemies too | That's true, it stops at eleven | Only teachers have them, at work | Frenemies are just a made-up word
test: Bella tells you «love ur hair» then whispers to her mates and they snigger. Which is most likely true? | She's being two-faced | She's just being nice to you | Her mates all have a bad cold | She was talking about the weather
exam: Identify the conduct which characterises a person commonly described as a «frenemy». | Friendly behaviour in private combined with unkindness before others | Consistent kindness in every setting | Consistent hostility in every setting | Friendship confined to pupils of other schools
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises teasing that is described by its author as «banter»? | It is banter only if all parties, including its subject, find it amusing | It is banter whenever its author so declares, irrespective of the subject's feelings | It is banter provided it occurs on school premises | It is banter whenever older pupils are present
exam: Given that a peer has distributed a private photograph of a pupil for the purpose of ridicule, ascertain the most appropriate course of action. | To request its removal and to inform a responsible adult | To distribute a more embarrassing photograph in return | To withdraw permanently from all group communication | To remain silent in the hope that it is forgotten
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning retaliation in kind against a two-faced friend. | It tends to produce two unhappy parties without resolving the matter | It invariably causes the offender to apologise | It is the recommended manner of restoring friendship | It increases the popularity of the retaliating pupil
